/* NEX-048b sanitizer fixture, NOT in test/behavior/manifest.txt: negating
   i32::MIN overflows (audit Site 4, native-executable-ub-audit.md) --
   expected UBSan verdict: "signed integer overflow" at the final
   negation. `min` itself is constructed without triggering any overflow
   (0 - 2147483647, then - 1, both representable at each step) so the
   deliberate UB is isolated to exactly the return expression. Observed
   (unsanitized, arm64/Apple Clang, -O0): silently wraps to INT32_MIN
   unchanged, exits 0 (INT32_MIN's low byte is 0) -- no crash on this
   platform; ARM's negate instruction doesn't trap on overflow, unlike
   x86, so this is exactly the kind of platform-dependent UB the audit
   flags rather than a cross-platform-safe assumption. Inert until
   NEX-050 wires a sanitizer job to run this file. */
fn main() -> i32 {
    let min: i32 = 0 - 2147483647 - 1;
    return 0 - min;
}
